---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.mjs';
---

<Base title="About" description="What this project is, what it is for, and how it is built in public.">
  <section>
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <h1>About</h1>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>A cycle-accurate 6502 and 65C02 core in C#, with the aim of implementing as many 6502-family machines on it as possible, each one proven. The design, the plan, the code and a journal of every step are public, in <a href={SITE.repo}>the repository</a>.</p>
        <h2>What proven means</h2>
        <p>The core is checked against Tom Harte's SingleStepTests, a published record of every opcode's bus activity, cycle by cycle, against whole programs that check their own results, and against a model of the chip's transistors. A machine counts as implemented only when it runs in the browser and passes an automated test in CI. Where the work knowingly differs from a reference, the <a href="/status/">status page</a> says so.</p>
        <h2>Nothing typed by hand</h2>
        <p>The figures on the home and status pages are read from the latest build's test results and from dated measurements. The build fails if a figure is missing.</p>
        <h2>Illustrations</h2>
        <p>The illustrations are generated, captioned as such, and never used as evidence. The die is a plausible one drawn by a model, not the 6502's real layout.</p>
        <h2>Licence</h2>
        <p>MIT, apart from what is somebody else's:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Each running machine's system ROMs are committed with where each copy came from and what is known of its rights. The site's build checks them against their hashes, and the machine's page says whose they are.</li>
          <li>Photographs of the original machines keep their sources' licences, named under each. Where a source states no licence, the credit says so.</li>
          <li>No commercial game is committed. What is committed is free software and homebrew whose authors let it be shared, each with its licence beside it: the BBC Micro's preset discs (games, demos and tools, one of them a disc of Dormann's and Clark's tests), the NES's Lan Master and the Atari 2600's 2048 2600.</li>
          <li>Any other game is the visitor's own, read in their browser and never uploaded.</li>
          <li>The tests download the test programs they run, pinned by hash.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
